<Base
  title="About · blackwall"
  description="Rahul, AI engineer leading tech and engineering at OneStop AI, building Jarvis/IRA, an agentic AI investing platform on WhatsApp, web, and mobile."
>
  <header class="post-header reveal">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="post-desc">Production war stories, with the numbers attached.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      I'm Rahul, a founding AI engineer with five-plus years in software, the last three shipping
      agentic LLM systems to production. Today I lead tech and engineering at
      <strong>OneStop AI</strong>, where I architect and own <strong>Jarvis/IRA</strong> end to
      end: an agentic AI investing platform on WhatsApp, web, and iOS/Android. The code lives in
      the <code>One-Stop-AI/osai_jango</code> repo. Everything I ship has to survive real users,
      real latency budgets, and real bills. Most of what I learned doing that isn't written down
      anywhere, so this site is me writing it down.
    </p>

    <h2>What I've shipped</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Jarvis, end to end.</strong> A Python/FastAPI agent backend with durable
        execution on SQS FIFO, a PostgreSQL inbox/outbox, and a singleton job scheduler on AWS
        ECS; cross-provider model routing with per-task backups, prompt caching, LLM-as-judge
        evals, and latency/cost telemetry; CI/CD with staging-on-merge and OTA plus store mobile
        releases. On top of it, a structured user memory card, mid-session context compaction
        and cross-session summaries, on-demand skills, multimodal portfolio intake (CSV, image,
        PDF), WhatsApp-to-app handoffs, and scheduled proactive investment alerts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A glucose forecaster</strong> built on a Transformer-LSTM hybrid, predicting
        spikes two hours ahead with 94% of predictions inside Clarke Zone A. The LLM extracts
        features. It doesn't tell fortunes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A vision pipeline for meal logging</strong> that handles ten thousand photos a
        day at a p90 of about six seconds, and lifted in-app logging from 24% to 38%.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An agent, judge, and extractor eval pipeline</strong> that scores production
        traces with compaction in the picture, because a benchmark that ignores how context
        degrades is measuring the wrong thing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prompt-cache engineering</strong> across multiple providers. A single timestamp
        in a system prompt once bought us a 0% cache hit rate. Once.
      </li>
      <li><strong>A Text-to-SQL agent</strong> with deterministic scoring. No AI in the math.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where I've worked</h2>
    <h3>OneStop AI <span class="when">Sep 2026 to present</span></h3>
    <p>
      Founding software engineer, leading tech and engineering. Lead engineer for Jarvis/IRA,
      the agentic AI investing platform described above, owned end to end with AI-accelerated
      development.
    </p>
    <h3>Canvas AI <span class="when">Nov 2023 to Aug 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Led three engineers delivering SugarFit's diabetes-care AI assistant for thousands of daily
      users: RAG to GraphRAG to QLoRA fine-tuning, a single agent to a multi-agent system, meal
      planning, symptom triage, CGM analysis, lab-report extraction, and a clinician copilot.
      Also built Apperture's datamart workbooks, eight-plus data connectors with CDC via Debezium
      and Kafka, and a WhatsApp Text-to-SQL BI agent at better than 90% NL-to-SQL accuracy.
    </p>
    <h3>Parallel <span class="when">Jul 2022 to Oct 2023</span></h3>
    <p>
      Product analyst, then backend engineer on Apperture's founding team. Built the B2B
      product-analytics platform's ClickHouse engines for funnels, retention, cohorts,
      segmentation, and custom metrics.
    </p>
    <h3>Quantile Analytics <span class="when">2021 to 2022</span></h3>
    <p>
      Quantitative analyst. Automated rebalancing-signal pipelines across twenty-plus global
      indices, and an NLP parser for SEC filings that replaced a lot of manual spreadsheet
      archaeology.
    </p>

    <h2>Education</h2>
    <p>B.Tech from IIT Indore, class of 2021.</p>
    <p>
      The fascination started there. Somewhere in the middle of my degree I took Andrew Ng's
      machine learning course on Coursera and fell hard for it, less for the demos than for the
      math underneath. Backpropagation, gradient descent, the perceptron. One assignment had me
      coding a CNN, and I still remember the small shock of watching it learn.
    </p>

    <h2>Receipts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>JEE Main 2017: rank 3,289 out of 1.2 million. Top 0.27%. JEE Advanced: rank 4,473.</li>
      <li>CAT 2021: 99.19 percentile. Chose to keep building instead.</li>
      <li>Bronze medal, BitGrit data science contest, Inter-IIT Tech Meet 2019.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>
      A place to share my work, what I'm learning, and the occasional war story. When a post
      makes a claim, there's a repo attached that backs it up.
    </p>
    <p>
      The name comes from Cyberpunk 2077. The <em>Blackwall</em> is the firewall that keeps rogue
      AIs away from everyone else's net, which felt about right for someone who ships agents for
      a living. The domain is a Gibson reference. Agent memory is the problem I care most about,
      and <em>Neuromancer</em> got there forty years early.
    </p>

